ear equivalence, just as the text outlines. That's another `faithful` one.\n\nClaim 8 has a subject that's an object (\"Stone space X\"), not a statement. Thus, the relation `proves` is wrong; it should be `has-property`. So, we're at `wrong-relation`.\n\nClaim 9 looks to me like a theorem statement again, and not a definition, so \"defines\" is incorrect. `wrong-relation`.\n\nClaim 10: Yup, the text explicitly proves that. `Faithful`.\n\nClaim 11: A direct equivalence is asserted in the text. `Faithful`.\n\nClaim 12: Matches the text exactly. `Faithful`.\n\nIn Claim 13, \"compact Hausdorff spaces\" is an object. Therefore the relation `proves` is wrong and it is `wrong-relation`.\n\nIn Claim 14, similar to Claim 3, the direction is wrong. Subject is the term, object is the content. That is `wrong-direction`.\n\nClaim 15: is clear and directly verifiable. `Faithful`.\n\nClaim 16, again, has an *object* as the subject, not a result. Thus `wrong-relation`.\n\nClaim 17 repeats the equivalence from Claim 11, so it is `faithful`.\n\nClaim 18, likewise, the subject \"X -> beta X assignment\" is an object so the relation is wrong. `wrong-relation`.\n\nClaim 19 is another `defines`, but with the term as subject. Therefore, this yields `wrong-direction` because the direction should be \"definition -> term\".\n\nFinally, Claim 20... The locator is \"Prop. 4.2.7\". However, that's a reference. The text actually includes \"Definition and Proposition . (cf. Def 4.2.2, Prop 4.2.7)\", so the claim isn't *asserted* at \"Prop 4.2.7\". Thus, we have a `wrong-locator`.\n\nOkay, I believe I have identified the first error for each of these claims!\n\n\n","thought":true,"thought_signature":null,"video_metadata":null,"tool_call":null,"tool_response":null,"part_metadata":null,"audio_transcription":null,"media_processing":null},{"media_resolution":null,"code_execution_result":null,"executable_code":null,"file_data":null,"function_call":null,"function_response":null,"inline_data":null,"text":"{\"n\": 1, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text states that \\\"The category Pro(C) is equivalent to the full subcategory of Fun(C, Set)^op whose objects are cofiltered limits of { Hom_C(X_i, -) }\\\".\"}\n{\"n\": 2, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The subject \\\"limits of compact Hausdorff spaces\\\" is a mathematical object, not a result, so this should be has-property instead of proves.\"}\n{\"n\": 3, \"grade\": \"wrong-direction\", \"reason\": \"The subject is the defined term and the object is the defining content, but the defines relation must point from the definition to the term.\"}\n{\"n\": 4, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The unnumbered theorem in the introduction states that extending the homotopy group functors to unique cofiltered limit preserving functors yields the required pro-groups.\"}\n{\"n\": 5, \"grade\": \"wrong-locator\", \"reason\": \"The equivalence is stated in an unnumbered Definition in the introduction that merely cites \\\"(cf. Prop. 2.1.2)\\\", so it is not asserted at the location \\\"Prop. 2.1.2\\\" itself.\"}\n{\"n\": 6, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Corollary 1.1.10 directly states that \\\"Every compact Hausdorff space admits a surjection from an extremally disconnected space.\\\"\"}\n{\"n\": 7, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Lemma 1.2.2 asserts that \\\"The small functors are precisely given by the small colimits of representables.\\\"\"}\n{\"n\": 8, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The subject \\\"Stone space X\\\" is an object, not a result, so it cannot be the subject of a proves relation.\"}\n{\"n\": 9, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The unnumbered theorem establishes the existence and uniqueness of the extensions, which is a mathematical statement (proves), not the introduction of a new term (defines).\"}\n{\"n\": 10, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Remark 1.1.6 establishes that \\\"Hausdorff extremally disconnected spaces are totally disconnected.\\\"\"}\n{\"n\": 11, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Proposition 1.2.12 states that \\\"The category ProFin of profinite sets is equivalent to the category Stone of Stone spaces\\\".\"}\n{\"n\": 12, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Proposition 1.1.1 explicitly states that \\\"Every surjection of compact Hausdorff spaces is a quotient map.\\\"\"}\n{\"n\": 13, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The subject \\\"compact Hausdorff spaces\\\" is a mathematical object rather than a result, so this should be has-property.\"}\n{\"n\": 14, \"grade\": \"wrong-direction\", \"reason\": \"The subject is the term \\\"condensed anima\\\" and the object is its definition, but the defines relation points from the definition to the term.\"}\n{\"n\": 15, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Lemma 1.1.4 establishes that \\\"Compact Hausdorff spaces are closed under finite coproducts and retracts.\\\"\"}\n{\"n\": 16, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The subject \\\"embedding Pro(Ani) -> Cond(Ani)\\\" is a mathematical object, not a result, so it cannot be the subject of a proves relation.\"}\n{\"n\": 17, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Proposition 1.2.12 asserts that \\\"The category ProFin of profinite sets is equivalent to the category Stone of Stone spaces\\\".\"}\n{\"n\": 18, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The \\\"X -> beta X assignment\\\" is a mathematical construction/object, not a result, so this should be has-property rather than proves.\"}\n{\"n\": 19, \"grade\": \"wrong-direction\", \"reason\": \"The subject is the defined term and the object is the defining content, which is the reverse of the required definition -> term direction for defines.\"}\n{\"n\": 20, \"grade\": \"wrong-locator\", \"reason\": \"The claim is based on an unnumbered Definition and Proposition in the introduction that cites \\\"(cf. Def. 4.2.2 , Prop. 4.2.7)\\\", rather than being asserted at the location Prop. 4.2.7 itself.\"}\nGRADES-END